<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Alignment</h1>
<p>Your alignment is how good or evil you are, from +1000 to -1000:
</p>
<pre>good       +350 to +1000
neutral    +349 to -349   (0 is true neutral)
evil       -350 to -1000
</pre>
<p>It changes mostly with what you kill:
</p>
<ul><li><b>Good</b> characters grow better by killing the evil, and lose much of their goodness by killing the good. Killing a neutral that leans to good costs them a little too.</li>
<li><b>Evil</b> characters sink deeper by killing the good.</li>
<li><b>Neutral</b> characters are pulled toward good when they kill something far more evil than themselves, and toward evil when they kill something far more good.</li></ul>
<p>The higher your level above your victim's, the bigger the change. Good is 
hard to keep and evil hard to leave, while staying neutral is easy. At -1000 
nothing you kill can make you better again.
</p><p>Alignment matters in many places. A good character earns a fifth more 
experience for killing the evil, an evil one a tenth more for killing the 
good. Some guilds take only good, or only evil, members (paladins must stay 
good, for instance), and many spells only work on, or against, one side: 
dispel evil, the holy and unholy words, protection from evil and good. The 
detect alignment spell shows others' alignment.
</p><p><br />
See Also:
</p>
